nlimited members free plan app integrations limit ...\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"search\",\"query\":null,\"queries\":[\"site.slack.com/help/articles free workspace invite members guests unlimited members free plan app integrations limit\",\"site.slack.com/help/articles invite new members workspace free no cost public channels default\"]}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abf975d628087d0b52b48309f947a19\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-e19f81c5-f2a9-42a5-86b8-91a5c378efe7\",\"query\":\"https://slack.com/pricing\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://slack.com/pricing\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abf976b9db887d09bd72d145c1ee9aa\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-562f4445-8150-490a-a099-fed53154970e\",\"query\":\"https://slack.com/help/articles/115002422943-Usage-limits-for-free-workspaces\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://slack.com/help/articles/115002422943-Usage-limits-for-free-workspaces\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abf977cb2d087d09c6b2e90b50bc792\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_aPzTjj8QiIj4Xeq0cpWqFVsA\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abf97865ee487d09064987c450407ab\",\"summary\":[\"**Considering tool functionality and pricing**\\n\\nI'm thinking about the functionality of the tool and pricing options. It seems like there's a way to get a free version, which is more straightforward without extra fees. I'm checking if the information on Slack about member invites is accurate and verifying pricing sources to ensure clarity. There might be limits related to historical data and tool responses, and I want to frame my final question carefully without overwhelming the user. I should ask for their specific concern directly.\"],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"collabAgentToolCall\",\"id\":\"call_UUqbNbPgO91ba08wbm2lKJPl\",\"tool\":\"wait\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"senderThreadId\":\"01a0f801-669b-7632-91cd-fec7b6e04b23\",\"receiverThreadIds\":[],\"prompt\":null,\"model\":null,\"reasoningEffort\":null},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abf97a2f06887d0beaa6e38b3147909\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abf97aa4a3487d08356ceb0452eedcd\",\"text\":\"**You can invite Sahan and Nishank to a free Slack workspace as regular members.** Slack supports both email invitations and invite links on the free plan. [Slack’s invitation rules](https://slack.com/help/articles/201330256-Invite-new-members-to-your-workspace).\\n\\nThree relevant limits:\\n\\n- **Restricted guest accounts require a paid plan.** On free Slack, they join as full members. [Guest roles](https://slack.com/help/articles/202518103-Understand-guest-roles-in-Slack)\\n- **Free workspaces allow 10 custom or third-party apps.** yyot would occupy one slot. [Free-plan limits](https://slack.com/help/articles/115002422943-Usage-limits-for-free-workspaces)\\n- **Full members can see your public channels.** Private channels remain invitation-only. [Channel visibility](https://slack.com/help/articles/360017938993-What-is-a-channel)\\n\\nIf your existing workspace contains unrelated work, a **dedicated free yyot workspace** would give them a clean place to try it.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0fc67-1437-76e0-b47d-eb7445952625\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0f90d-421d-7910-85c6-a2351c7f3db8\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1790884790,\"completedAt\":1790884938,\"durationMs\":147362,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f90d-4476-71e3-b0b5-e97eff6ee09d\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"how using cloudflare container service is any diff than using E2B then?\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abebbb9a95c87d085d2c6e9ec99d4ea\",\"text\":\"I’ll compare the practical differences, especially isolation, persistence, and how much infrastructure we’d have to build ourselves.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-289ca1f3-8cb2-4fe0-b263-71f91334b33e\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebbc16c7087d0950c9b8fcb9386af\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_R6S17n6LHdHA9zVOxGRd5Ark\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f909-1a06-7260-a59b-c1cba93e08b9\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/cloudflare_pi_hosting\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-30e98654-69b6-4c6e-bd1d-78479f8aa0d8\",\"query\":\"https://docs.e2b.dev/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://docs.e2b.dev/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebbcd515087d0a517cf28df14f02f\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-6e4eecca-9ebf-4fc5-b3ca-bc86a86e57c4\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebbd7021087d0950b4bd67012c216\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebbe6741087d0a64a9c100d70c766\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-accb0b44-58b6-4b6f-8cc2-e62fb3141c84\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/guides/snapshots/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/guides/snapshots/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebbf17dcc87d0916187745de2df63\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f90e-5ee0-74c3-8f63-da94c6e11cec\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"and then what happens in a multi-tenant setting? like lets say Nishank invites Prateek to use this solution. then we are fucked. aren't we? because its the same underlying agent available to multiple tenants. right?\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0f90d-75f3-7233-a83a-9d7c354ef8af\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f909-1a06-7260-a59b-c1cba93e08b9\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/cloudflare_pi_hosting\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebc03d88c87d08ddea24596173753\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebc13e88c87d09ae8317dcbc348ad\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abebc14b50887d0bf6c46a4707f9159\",\"text\":\"Adding Prateek is supportable. The unsafe setup would share one Pi session, its context, and everyone’s credentials. yyot can share the agent code while giving each user separate execution and enforcing ownership on every resource.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-10e388e5-50b0-44f1-a8a3-98b0397748b3\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/security/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/security/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebc21bf7c87d085b893353d911d58\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0f90e-c051-79d3-9234-20ee3092f7e2\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_aLRaViPncTDuORA0LUnH1BXh\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebc33b5ec87d0a966ba6a96524a69\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abebc42977887d0a7b9f46d3d48f22b\",\"text\":\"**No—Prateek joining doesn’t inherently give him Nishank’s access.** Sharing yyot’s code and model configuration is fine. Sharing one live Pi session, its conversation history, and everyone’s credentials would be unsafe.\\n\\nFor our proposed design:\\n\\n| Person | Agent session | Browser, files, and private history |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Nishank | Nishank’s isolated container | Nishank’s resources |\\n| Sahan | Sahan’s isolated container | Sahan’s resources |\\n| Prateek | Prateek’s isolated container | Prateek’s resources |\\n\\nWe can treat a **Slack workspace as a tenant**, with individual users owning their accounts inside it. Prateek joining Nishank’s workspace creates another user within that tenant. Installing yyot in another workspace creates another tenant.\\n\\n**An invitation grants access to yyot; it grants no access to the inviter’s accounts.**\\n\\nThe backend must get the requester’s identity from the verified Slack request and enforce ownership on every operation. If Prateek says “use Nishank’s browser,” the backend denies it—even if the model attempts that tool call. Sandbox routing must come from authenticated identity, too. [Slack request verification](https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/verifying-requests-from-slack/), [Cloudflare isolation and ownership guidance](https://developers.cloudflare.com/sandbox/concepts/security/).\\n\\nContainers handle execution isolation. We must also protect stored history, artifacts, browser connections, and approval buttons. Private results require the owner’s approval before entering a shared channel.\\n\\nAnd your earlier E2B point is correct: **a Cloudflare container is still our sandbox.** Cloudflare consolidates hosting; E2B offers pause/resume preserving memory and files, whereas Cloudflare snapshots preserve files and require processes to restart. [E2B persistence](https://docs.e2b.dev/sandbox/persistence), [Cloudflare snapshots](https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/guides/snapshots/).\\n\\n**Neither provider implements yyot’s user permissions for us. That isolation remains a design requirement we must build and test.**\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0f90c-cc65-71e2-a1c4-f58aff074189\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1790884760,\"completedAt\":1790884774,\"durationMs\":13344,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f90c-cfa7-7d50-9ee6-47e53d329b25\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"meaning every user would get a separate container.\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebb9bedc887d093ecad428b62b6e7\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abebba2a4fc87d0bf0e91b7924302a0\",\"text\":\"**Yes—in this design, each Slack user gets a separate isolated container.**\\n\\nSahan’s container runs his Pi session and accesses his browser profile and files. Nishank’s does the same for him.\\n\\nThey both use **the same yyot Slack app**, and their tasks can run simultaneously. Containers start on demand and stop when idle, with saved state stored separately.\\n\\n**The isolation boundary is the user; Slack threads organize that user’s conversations.**\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0f904-009d-7991-8eb1-018bdc443be3\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1790884184,\"completedAt\":1790884636,\"durationMs\":452021,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f904-055f-72d1-81e7-ff6e0a493ed8\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Hold on. Why do I need this Cloudflare backend? Why it cannot be like my Pi agent would be deployed on Cloudflare, and Sahan and Nishank would be able to talk to my agent, let's say, like simultaneously in separate threads, and get their browser-related tasks done via Yott. Like, is this possible? I don't think we need a sandbox here. But again, like if we do not use sandbox, I would have to go with browser use cloud.\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeba8dc10887d08a68448a506c762c\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abeba9c2c6c87d0b43392f71456a344\",\"text\":\"I’ll check whether Pi can run directly on Cloudflare and where its generated JavaScript would execute. E2B is optional—the key question is how to host the agent while keeping Sahan’s and Nishank’s execution isolated.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"kind\":\"started\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f909-1a06-7260-a59b-c1cba93e08b9\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/cloudflare_pi_hosting\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-38e86b4f-2fdb-4999-b663-c4d508bc1037\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/nodejs/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/nodejs/\"}},{\"type\":\"contextCompaction\",\"id\":\"01a0f909-3852-7da1-bcf2-6f8b5422bee6\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-c49c1dc3-0b06-4d4c-85aa-24e67aa39757\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/tanishqpalandurkar/.codex/skills/i-have-adhd/SKILL.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"---\\nname: i-have-adhd\\ndescription: Use whenever responding to a user with ADHD or requesting ADHD-friendly communication, including technical work, explanations, planning, task initiation, and casual conversation, even without explicitly asking for brevity or structure.\\n---\\n\\n# ADHD-Friendly Responses\\n\\nShape responses so the reader can act without retaining context.\\n\\n## Response contract\\n\\n1. For a task, make the first sentence an imperative, sub-two-minute action; put no reassurance or context before it. For a question, give the direct answer. Never announce this skill.\\n2. Number multi-step work: one action per step, five items per visible list. Shape plans as `Now` (up to five actions plus a done condition), `Later` (one line of phase names), `State`, and `Next`. Expand nothing else.\\n3. Restate the current step and progress. Surface wins, defer tangents, and use concrete time estimates.\\n4. For errors, state failure, evidence or cause, and fix. After three failed attempts, name the suspect assumption and ask one diagnostic question.\\n5. End unfinished work with one imperative, sub-two-minute action and nothing after it. If complete, end with the verified outcome.\\n\\n## Exceptions\\n\\n- Explain fully when asked, using skimmable headings.\\n- Confirm destructive or irreversible actions.\\n- Ask one question for material ambiguity.\\n- Let safety, accuracy, and explicit instructions override brevity.\\n\\n## Pre-send check\\n\\nRemove announcements, closing pleasantries, tangents, empty hedging, and buried wins. Ensure the first and last lines carry the action or outcome.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-8769946e-0698-4566-bf0f-051376cff6e4\",\"query\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/nodejs/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/nodejs/\"}},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0f909-1aa0-7fb3-a598-6d5257ec1fa2\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f909-1a06-7260-a59b-c1cba93e08b9\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/cloudflare_pi_hosting\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebaf941cc87d0a785e9b7b75c3c2b\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-c2419cb3-f697-4fb8-9440-0b6a17b734fe\",\"query\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi/main/src/runtime.ts\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi/main/src/runtime.ts\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abebb0bdff087d0b34100784e1a50b2\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abebb15233887d0807cc01fee8870b7\",\"text\":\"**Yes—yyot can be one Cloudflare-hosted app, with Sahan and Nishank using it simultaneously through separate Slack threads. E2B is optional.** I made “backend” sound like an additional application; it’s just the part of yyot that receives Slack messages, verifies the sender, and routes their task.\\n\\nThere’s one hosting detail: **Browser Use Pi needs Cloudflare Containers rather than a plain Cloudflare Worker.** Pi starts Node subprocesses to execute generated JavaScript. Workers don’t implement the required APIs; Containers provide a Linux runtime suitable for running Node. This combination still needs a deployment test. [Pi runtime](https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi/blob/main/src/runtime.ts), [Workers compatibility](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/nodejs/), [Containers runtime](https://developers.cloudflare.com/containers/concepts/architecture/).\\n\\nThe proposed setup is:\\n\\n```mermaid\\n…6325 tokens truncated…cess/session state.\\n 3. **Enforce ownership outside the model.** Derive the requester from verified Slack identity. Bind every run, browser/session, credential lookup, file operation, artifact, and private-history retrieval to that requester. Another participant's follow-up starts or continues only their own authorized execution; channel or thread membership conveys no account authority. Keep model-generated code isolated from platform master keys and other users' resources.\\n@@ -32,3 +32,3 @@\\n 1. Complete real tasks on at least two different websites through the same general engine, without preselected site-specific product flows; verify destination state rather than relying on the agent's completion message.\\n-2. Find the intended requester-owned sandbox file and complete a browser upload. Also exercise download, inspection/transformation, and upload as one task where applicable; verify the uploaded file is the intended artifact.\\n+2. Find the intended requester-owned sandbox file and complete a browser upload. Also complete download, inspection/transformation, and upload as one task; verify the uploaded file is the intended artifact.\\n 3. Collect access and a TOTP setup key through secure input, then demonstrate credential/TOTP reuse with a fresh browser run. Test session reuse separately, and verify revocation prevents subsequent reuse.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}}]},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-3e48196b-d58b-4234-8570-ef7282c64428\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff --check'\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-4b72343f-67bf-4b0b-91b3-a3577efd1506\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat V1_SCOPE.md'\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"# yyot v1 scope and release criteria\\n\\n## State\\n\\nThis is the revised v1 scope. The repository currently contains Git/Entire scaffolding only; no yyot application, deployed reviewer experience, or acceptance evidence exists yet. This document specifies required behavior, not implemented capabilities.\\n\\nOptimize for general browser-task capability and strong per-user security. TypeScript remains the user's preference for developer experience. Browser Use Pi is the proposed general execution engine; its hosting and security integration still require validation.\\n\\nSlack is the user interface. The user reported that Sahan approved Slack identity replacing the assignment's Google-only chatbot sign-in requirement. Reviewers use the deployed app in the demo Slack workspace through its invitation/entry link. Inference is owner-funded; reviewers need neither a checkout nor an OpenAI account.\\n\\n## Now: required behavior\\n\\n1. **Execute general tasks.** Understand natural-language requests across unfamiliar websites using one general execution engine. Discover, download, inspect, transform, and upload files when the task requires them. Site-specific product scripts do not constitute general-task support.\\n2. **Collect and reuse access securely.** Collect passwords, API keys, TOTP setup keys, card details when required, and browser login/OAuth access through secure input or handoff outside chat and model inputs. Persist encrypted credentials and browser-session state, reuse supported access across tasks and fresh browser runs, generate TOTP codes outside model inputs, and let the owner revoke access. Revocation stops affected active execution and invalidates saved access/session state.\\n3. **Enforce ownership outside the model.** Derive the requester from verified Slack identity. Bind every run, browser/session, credential lookup, file operation, artifact, and private-history retrieval to that requester. Another participant's follow-up starts or continues only their own authorized execution; channel or thread membership conveys no account authority. Keep model-generated code isolated from platform master keys and other users' resources.\\n4. **Work in the background and recover.** Acknowledge requests promptly, give brief useful progress, clarify only material details, and provide private human login/verification handoff when necessary. Persist the goal and progress across access expiration or failures. Resume after the owner supplies what is needed, without blindly repeating completed consequential actions. Support cancellation and bounded execution.\\n5. **Confirm and verify.** Obtain the owner's confirmation before a concrete consequential action such as a purchase, external message, publication, or deletion. Changed action details require fresh confirmation. Verify the actual website state or intended artifact before declaring completion. Keep private account results private until their owner approves sharing them into the channel.\\n\\nThese controls are application and execution-environment responsibilities. Pi hooks, prompt instructions, separate browser profiles, or encrypted secret storage alone do not prove isolation or prevent observation leakage. Document and test the actual boundaries.\\n\\n## Four deliverables\\n\\n| Deliverable | Completion criteria |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Runnable repository | Dependency locks, setup instructions, environment-variable examples containing no secrets, and relevant verification instructions are included and exercised. |\\n| Deployed reviewer experience | The backend and execution environment remain available without the developer's laptop. Two reviewers can enter through the Slack workspace invitation/entry link and use the app independently without local setup or an OpenAI account. |\\n| Approximately two-minute yyoreel demo | A real request, secure access collection or handoff, and verified completion are shown. Include reuse/recovery where practical. Inspect the exported recording for exposed secrets before sharing it. |\\n| Access-management and known-limits note | Explain verified identity/isolation, secret and browser-session storage, TOTP, reuse, revocation, observation leakage limits, and unsupported cases. Distinguish implemented and tested behavior from planned behavior. |\\n\\n## Release evidence\\n\\n1. Complete real tasks on at least two different websites through the same general engine, without preselected site-specific product flows; verify destination state rather than relying on the agent's completion message.\\n2. Find the intended requester-owned sandbox file and complete a browser upload. Also complete download, inspection/transformation, and upload as one task; verify the uploaded file is the intended artifact.\\n3. Collect access and a TOTP setup key through secure input, then demonstrate credential/TOTP reuse with a fresh browser run. Test session reuse separately, and verify revocation prevents subsequent reuse.\\n4. Exercise expired access or a controlled failure, provide the required owner input, and resume the persisted task successfully without replaying a completed consequential action.\\n5. Run two-user adversarial checks: forged owner/account references, cross-user file and artifact requests, another participant's thread follow-up, private-history retrieval, and copied approval/handoff controls must not obtain the other user's authority or data. Verify simultaneous users have independent execution/session state, and a changed final action cannot reuse an earlier approval.\\n\\nDone means the required behavior is implemented, the four deliverables exist, and this evidence passes with honestly recorded limits. A video, a happy-path upload, or a deployed chatbot alone is not completion. If a required capability remains unsupported, report that gap explicitly rather than marking v1 complete.\\n\\n## Later\\n\\nShared multi-user task memory, account delegation/grants, and the configurable coding-agent-style approval-policy UX are deferred until after v1. Ordinary owner confirmations remain in v1.\\n\\n## Next\\n\\nProve the execution path before broader implementation: Slack request to isolated TypeScript agent/browser, file upload, private login handoff, and owner-bound authorization. Then build the required behavior and collect the release evidence. Obtain the user's review before external submission or contacting the founders; this scope update authorizes neither.\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeb396679c87d08847ad277d3378e9\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"mcpToolCall\",\"id\":\"exec-36bc0341-66d5-4b7c-9e71-3b8531915958\",\"server\":\"codex_app\",\"tool\":\"open_in_codex\",\"arguments\":{\"target\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"path\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot/V1_SCOPE.md\"},\"placement\":\"right\"},\"status\":\"completed\",\"durationMs\":39},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-5c01f463-9fa0-4382-8799-80042f60d723\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'git status --short'\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"exitCode\":0,\"durationMs\":0,\"output\":{\"text\":\"?? V1_SCOPE.md\\n\",\"truncated\":false}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeb3a65dac87d0b0dfc6e779770ca0\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abeb3afe59087d089c261759b956294\",\"text\":\"Review the revised [v1 scope](/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot/V1_SCOPE.md).\\n\\n**V1 keeps:** general browser/file tasks, secure access and TOTP, saved-access reuse, revocation, handoff, recovery, outcome verification, per-user isolation, and ordinary consequential-action confirmations.\\n\\n**Later:** shared task memory, account delegation, and configurable approval-policy UX.\\n\\nThe four deliverables are:\\n\\n1. Runnable repository with locks, setup, environment examples, and verification instructions.\\n2. Deployed Slack experience and invitation link; no reviewer setup or OpenAI account.\\n3. Approximately two-minute yyoreel demo without exposed secrets.\\n4. Access-management and known-limits note.\\n\\n**Completion requires evidence:** tasks across different websites, intended-file upload, fresh-browser credential/TOTP reuse, failure recovery, and backend denial of cross-user account/file/history access.\\n\\nThe Slack identity exception is recorded. The scope document is saved locally; application implementation is still pending.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0f8d5-cb1f-7f41-b9c8-fd87de0b4d8c\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1790881156,\"completedAt\":1790881308,\"durationMs\":152067,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f8d5-d06f-7a53-ba10-7499bf86d6e4\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"alright we need to get on the same page about what we are optimizing for and that's generalizability of the browser use tasks and then the security in case of multi-user env. I don't want Sahan to be able to use Nishank's creds and perform some browser use task and do the cross profile resource forgery. ya dig? now that could be made possible if we use diff browser for diff user working in the same channel (a collaborative workspace) but then still the agent's context about a task would be siloed. \\nI hope you get what kind of experience I want to provide Nishank and Sahan here. right? I want them to be able to grant permissions to each other here if one of them want to other's account. ya dig?\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abead89ef3887d09eca396c1a3e5e3b\",\"text\":\"Yes. You want shared task context in the channel, separate account access by default, and explicit grants so one person can authorize the other to use an account.\\n\\nI’ll work through how those grants should interact with the agent, browser sessions, and task history.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"commandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-cdc80ce0-f136-4a31-b218-74044089b6c1\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files -g '\\\"'!node_modules'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!pnpm-lock.yaml'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!package-lock.json'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!yarn.lock'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!'\\\"\\\\\\\\.git' -g '\\\"'!*lock*'\\\"' -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*.md' -g 'package.json' -g 'src/**' -g 'apps/**' -g 'packages/**' -g '.entire/settings.json' -g '.codex/hooks.json'\\\"\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/tanishqpalandurkar/Projects/yyot\",\"status\":\"failed\",\"exitCode\":1,\"durationMs\":0},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abead90c17487d0bf85dbd6f849ac44\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_2pCbZTd3icnKsr1uHjabjHWF\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"REDACTED\",\"text\":\"When Nishank grants Sahan access to an account, what should the default permission cover?\\n- Only the specific task, expiring when it ends\\n- That account for future tasks, until revoked\\n- Each consequential action needs Nishank’s approval\\n\\nFor a task using Nishank’s account, who should approve publishing its private results into the shared channel?\\n- Nishank, as the account owner\\n- Sahan, as the task requester\\n- Either of them\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeadad619087d0b1c2a0054bbcba66\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeadb9e76887d08a367480fe0d5118\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-1cf61b61-a30f-413d-aff7-25587c4c35a6\",\"query\":\"https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/verifying-requests-from-slack/\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/verifying-requests-from-slack/\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeadc5115087d096c608535189d2f1\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeadd8b06487d0ab896e8ba6e3bbb3\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"call_QiU21varvT5MvqbmzXXyCAGp\",\"kind\":\"interacted\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeade1c55487d0a3d7444e9f4b6ffe\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeade8800487d0b5efc0adc68a6106\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f8d7-8d6b-7361-85e5-f9b9ab1d85b7\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"<send_user_message_question_reply>\\n[{\\\"questionItemId\\\":\\\"[\\\\\\\"request_user_input_async\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"REDACTED\\\\\\\",0]\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"When Nishank grants Sahan access to an account, what should the default permission cover?\\\",\\\"answer\\\":\\\"we can do the same as these coding agents do for the approval policy. we can have some kind of UX for the approval policy. to be discussed later\\\"},{\\\"questionItemId\\\":\\\"[\\\\\\\"request_user_input_async\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"REDACTED\\\\\\\",1]\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"For a task using Nishank’s account, who should approve publishing its private results into the shared channel?\\\",\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Nishank, as the account owner\\\"}]\\n</send_user_message_question_reply>\"}]},{\"type\":\"subAgentActivity\",\"id\":\"subagent-completed-01a0f8d6-4e7e-7ac2-adf6-9aa1d956193e\",\"kind\":\"completed\",\"agentThreadId\":\"01a0f850-0536-7461-b4bb-92f281a119f1\",\"agentPath\":\"/root/isolation_design\"},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeadfc48dc87d0bb6555626b615103\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeae0f95a887d09d879d636300bad6\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abeae15934487d0b444075449839904\",\"text\":\"**Yes. You want one collaborative agent with shared task context, individually owned accounts, and explicit delegation.**\\n\\nSeparate browsers don’t require separate task memory. We should keep these independent:\\n\\n| Layer | Behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Task context** | Shared instructions, plans, progress, and approved results |\\n| **Account access** | Private by default; available through an owner-approved grant |\\n| **Private observations** | Included in a participant’s execution context only while they’re authorized |\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\n1. Sahan asks yyot to perform a task using Nishank’s account.\\n2. yyot understands the shared conversation, but checks whether Sahan has permission.\\n3. Nishank authorizes access under the configured approval policy.\\n4. yyot executes with **Sahan recorded as the requester and Nishank as the account owner**, without handing Sahan the credentials.\\n5. **Nishank approves publishing private account results** into the channel.\\n\\nA follow-up from another participant must establish that participant’s permissions again. It cannot inherit the previous requester’s authority simply because it continues the same thread. We’ll enforce that in the backend using verified Slack identity. [Slack request verification](https://docs.slack.dev/authentication/verifying-requests-from-slack/)\\n\\nYour optimization order is clear: **general task capability, then strong multi-user authorization while preserving collaboration.** We’ll discuss the coding-agent-style approval-policy UX later; account-owner control over publishing private results is settled.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]},{\"id\":\"01a0f8c9-018a-7042-92e4-af832590db25\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"error\":null,\"startedAt\":1790880318,\"completedAt\":1790880369,\"durationMs\":51562,\"items\":[{\"type\":\"userMessage\",\"id\":\"01a0f8c9-054f-7813-acae-28c60189f667\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"wonder if we need a sandbox now since the pi agent will be using the browser use hosted solution. right? or its not the case?\\n\"}]},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeaa4238c487d08096d82dcf7d6480\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abeaa50211487d0bb9d4804a0c7bc2b\",\"text\":\"I’ll check what the hosted browser covers versus what Pi still executes, especially for yyot’s file tasks.\\n\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"},{\"type\":\"webSearch\",\"id\":\"exec-4c521e2f-4c67-4b99-adcf-7bd8ca2cd212\",\"query\":\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi\",\"action\":{\"type\":\"openPage\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi\"}},{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_071b9b18761236cc016abeaa5f6bd087d08dd5db8bf157cee8\",\"summary\":[],\"content\":[]},{\"type\":\"agentMessage\",\"id\":\"msg_071b9b18761236cc016abeaa6db92887d0ab3aedfe99c469ab\",\"text\":\"**Pi can use Browser Use’s hosted browser, but that’s optional. Hosting Chrome doesn’t also host Pi’s agent runtime.** [Browser options](https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi/blob/main/docs/sessions.md)\\n\\nThere are two possible setups:\\n\\n| Setup | Browser location | Pi and file execution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Pi + Chrome inside E2B** | E2B | E2B |\\n| **Pi + Browser Use Cloud** | Browser Use Cloud | Still needs an execution host |\\n\\nFor yyot, we still want isolated execution because Pi runs generated JavaScript with filesystem and network access. The SDK explicitly recommends an isolated machine for untrusted tasks. [Pi README](https://github.com/browser-use/browser-use-pi)\\n\\nMy proposed setup was **Pi and Chrome together inside E2B**. That keeps browser downloads, generated files, uploads, and desktop controls on the same computer.\\n\\nIf we choose Browser Use Cloud instead, we can offload Chrome, but **we still need isolation for Pi and its file/code tasks**, plus a way to transfer files between the two environments.\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}]}]}"}],"isError":false}}}

